skip

CAPTURE INSTRUMENT, no graded answer. It bails one Abrams crew, photographs them four times from a
fixed camera as they walk off the west edge, and asserts nothing about whether they got there.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS. The Lua evaluates no predicate about the mod. Its checks -- that the hull
resolved and reports its authored north facing, that the requested zoom was not clamped away, and
that three crew are alive at each shutter -- are guards on the INSTRUMENT, not measurements of the
subject. They can only downgrade the frames to "not evidence". A Test.Pass would contribute a green
to every run-batch.sh --all tally whether or not the capture landed, which is the mislabelling
corrected across this suite on 2026-09-01.

The behaviour is already graded next door. test-crew-auto-evacuate asserts that crew left alone
leave the world and that the ONE man handed a real Move order is still standing where he was sent --
the one-shot, freely-overridable semantics, which no picture can show.

Note that frame 04 is deliberately allowed to come back empty of infantry: that means the crew
crossed the boundary before the shutter, which is the feature working. A scenario that FAILED on
that would be asserting a schedule, not a behaviour.

A PASS against a live `skip` declaration grades RED on purpose, so a scenario that starts grading
announces itself instead of silently changing meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail` declarations, where a
watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green. A timeout reaches
expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED.
